From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Theorem Labs
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 19:55:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [compiler-rt] silent reports: a runtime that dies without
 writing
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

🤖: Sealed init (patch 0012) made the runtime's start-up a function of the
binary alone, and left the report path out on purpose: "a run that reports an
error is not sealed and is not meant to be."  That is the right default and
the wrong last word for one consumer.  An equivalence statement between a
binary and its sanitized twin quantifies over all inputs, the inputs on which
the sanitizer fires included, and on those a loud runtime writes a report the
plain binary has no counterpart for -- so the write syscalls that carry it
are a difference a syscall-level model has to either model or excuse.  What
that consumer needs is a runtime that still detects, still dies, and dies
with the same exit status, but writes nothing on the way down: the behaviour
the same binary would have if stderr had been closed before it started, minus
the failing write itself.

Report text leaves the process through exactly two doors, and this patch puts
the same test behind both.  ReportFile::Write is where everything Printf(),
Report() and RawWrite() produce funnels -- the report body, verbosity output,
statistics, the leak checker's summary -- and the test sits before the lock
and before ReopenIfNecessary(), so a silent process never opens a log file
either.  CatastrophicErrorWrite is the other door, the one CHECK failures and
Die() use when the report file cannot be trusted, and it writes to kStderrFd
directly.  Nothing else in the posix runtime writes report text: the other
internal_write callers are the pipe probes (IsAccessibleMemoryRange,
TryMemCpy), which write to their own pipes and are not reports.

What is deliberately unchanged: detection, halt_on_error, Die(), the exit
status, the death callback, and the program's own output -- this silences the
runtime, not the process.  Also unchanged is the work of building the report:
unwinding and symbolization still run and still cost their syscalls, so a
caller who needs the error path to make no syscalls at all needs more than
this.  The silence begins where the bytes would have left the process.

The switch is the same mechanism as the seal and for the same reason
(sanitizer_sealed.h): an undefined weak symbol the link may define,

  -Wl,--defsym=__sanitizer_silent_reports=1

so one runtime archive serves both modes, a silent binary and its loud
control differ in one linker flag, and the two symbols compose -- a sealed
silent binary is both defsyms on the same link.

---
 .../lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_file.cpp   |  6 ++
 .../lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_posix.cpp  |  7 ++
 .../lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_silent.h   | 92 +++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 105 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_silent.h

diff --git a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_file.cpp b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_file.cpp
index 96af270..e35823e 100644
--- a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_file.cpp
+++ b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_file.cpp
@@ -19,11 +19,17 @@
 
 #include "sanitizer_common.h"
 #include "sanitizer_file.h"
+#include "sanitizer_silent.h"
 #  include "sanitizer_interface_internal.h"
 
 namespace __sanitizer {
 
 void CatastrophicErrorWrite(const char *buffer, uptr length) {
+  // The one path to stderr that does not go through ReportFile::Write:
+  // CHECK failures and Die() use this when the report file cannot be
+  // trusted.  Same test, same meaning; see sanitizer_silent.h.
+  if (SilentReports())
+    return;
   WriteToFile(kStderrFd, buffer, length);
 }
 
diff --git a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_posix.cpp b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_posix.cpp
index e80d495..b9fe40c 100644
--- a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_posix.cpp
+++ b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_posix.cpp
@@ -22,6 +22,7 @@
 #include "sanitizer_posix.h"
 #include "sanitizer_procmaps.h"
 #include "sanitizer_sealed.h"
+#include "sanitizer_silent.h"
 
 #include <errno.h>
 #include <fcntl.h>
@@ -293,6 +294,12 @@ bool IsAbsolutePath(const char *path) {
 }
 
 void ReportFile::Write(const char *buffer, uptr length) {
+  // Everything Printf(), Report() and RawWrite() emit funnels through here,
+  // so this one test is most of what "silent" means; see sanitizer_silent.h.
+  // The return is before the lock and before ReopenIfNecessary(), so a
+  // silent process also never opens a log file.
+  if (SilentReports())
+    return;
   SpinMutexLock l(mu);
   ReopenIfNecessary();
   internal_write(fd, buffer, length);
diff --git a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_silent.h b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_silent.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..6a1fd74
--- /dev/null
+++ b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_silent.h
@@ -0,0 +1,92 @@
+//===-- sanitizer_silent.h --------------------------------------*- C++ -*-===//
+//
+// Part of the LLVM Project, under the Apache License v2.0 with LLVM Exceptions.
+// See https://llvm.org/LICENSE.txt for license information.
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception
+//
+//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
+//
+// Silent reports: a link-time mode in which the runtime writes nothing, to
+// stderr or anywhere else.  The process behaves as if stderr had been closed
+// before it started: an error is still detected, the process still dies, and
+// it dies with the same exit status it would have died with -- what is gone
+// is the bytes, and the write syscalls that would have carried them.
+//
+// It is off unless the link defines __sanitizer_silent_reports, so nothing
+// here changes what an ordinary build does.  What it changes when it is on:
+//
+//   - ReportFile::Write() returns before writing.  Everything Printf(),
+//     Report(), VReport() and RawWrite() produce -- the report body, the
+//     shadow legend, verbosity output, statistics, the leak checker's
+//     summary -- funnels through that one function, so one test there is
+//     what makes this total rather than a list of message kinds.  The
+//     return is also before ReopenIfNecessary(), so a silent process never
+//     opens a log file either: log_path names a file that is not created.
+//   - CatastrophicErrorWrite() returns before writing.  It is the one other
+//     path to stderr: CHECK failures and Die() use it when the report file
+//     cannot be trusted, and it writes to kStderrFd directly.
+//
+// What it does NOT change, on purpose:
+//
+//   - detection.  The checks run, the error is found, and halt_on_error
+//     still decides what happens next.  A silent binary is not a binary
+//     with the sanitizer weakened; it is one whose verdict arrives as an
+//     exit status instead of as text.
+//   - the exit status.  Die() runs exactly as before -- flags()->exitcode,
+//     abort_on_error, the death callback -- so a silent binary and a loud
+//     binary built from the same source leave the same corpse.  A checker
+//     that reads the exit status cannot tell them apart; only one that
+//     reads stderr can, which is the point.
+//   - the program's own output.  This silences the runtime, not the
+//     process: printf and fprintf(stderr, ...) in the program still write.
+//     "As if stderr were closed" describes the runtime's relationship to
+//     its report stream, not the file descriptor itself, which is left
+//     exactly as the program received it.
+//   - the work of building the report.  Unwinding, symbolization and the
+//     rest of the error path still run and still cost what they cost,
+//     syscalls included; the silence begins where the bytes would have
+//     left the process.  A caller who needs the error path to make no
+//     syscalls at all needs halt_on_error's earliest exit, not this.
+//
+// The switch is a symbol rather than a build option for the same reason
+// sealed init's is (sanitizer_sealed.h): one runtime archive serves both
+// modes, so a silent binary and its loud control can be built and compared
+// inside one image, differing in one linker flag:
+//
+//   -Wl,--defsym=__sanitizer_silent_reports=1
+//
+// and it composes with the seal the same way -- the two symbols are
+// independent, so any configuration can add either or both.
+//
+// Why anyone wants this.  An equivalence statement between a binary and its
+// sanitized twin quantifies over inputs, including the inputs on which the
+// sanitizer fires; on those, a loud runtime writes a report the plain binary
+// has no counterpart for, and the write syscalls that carry it are exactly
+// the difference a syscall-level model then has to excuse.  Silencing the
+// report makes the sanitized binary's failure mode what the statement needs
+// it to be: the same syscalls, then a different exit status.  The exit
+// status is the entire channel, and it is a channel the plain binary also
+// has.
+//
+//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
+
+#ifndef SANITIZER_SILENT_H
+#define SANITIZER_SILENT_H
+
+#include "sanitizer_internal_defs.h"
+
+extern "C" {
+// Defined by the link, as an absolute symbol, when silent reports are wanted,
+// and left as an undefined weak symbol, whose address is 0, when they are not.
+SANITIZER_WEAK_ATTRIBUTE extern char __sanitizer_silent_reports;
+}
+
+namespace __sanitizer {
+
+// Decided by the link and never changes afterwards, so this is a constant to
+// every caller and the branches below fold away in each run.
+inline bool SilentReports() { return &__sanitizer_silent_reports != nullptr; }
+
+}  // namespace __sanitizer
+
+#endif  // SANITIZER_SILENT_H
-- 
2.39.5

